cartridge "RAID/LARRY COATS/1985"

' RAID
' A ColecoVision game based on "RAID" by Larry Coats, Ahoy! May 1985
' (Commodore 64: a BASIC program with 1,309 bytes of machine code in DATA
' lines at $C100, "(C) 1983 BY LARRY COATS" on its instruction screen;
' orig/ has the program, its detokenized listing and a disassembly of the
' machine code). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic),
' 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' From the original (rules read from the BASIC and the machine code):
' a walled field of four tall rooms joined by gaps; 29 hearts in fixed
' places, 16 butterflies starting in fixed places, the raider in a pocket
' on the left of the middle row, the exit at the far end of the pocket on
' the right. The raider moves one cell while the stick is held, in eight
' directions; a diagonal blocked by a wall slides along it (the vertical
' way tried first, then the horizontal, into a heart or an empty cell
' only). Walking into a butterfly is death. Each butterfly step, every
' butterfly (the last one first) looks at its four neighbours: the raider
' there and it pounces (death); else it tries one random way of four and
' moves only into an empty cell (never onto a heart, a wall or another
' butterfly). The raider and the butterflies take turns at the same pace.
' Hearts: 10 points each, 100 more for taking the last one; walking out on
' the right with every heart taken: 500 and the next level (the hearts all
' back). Hearts taken stay taken after a death; the butterflies go back to
' their places. Five lives. The skill level 1-9 is the starting level, and
' every level is faster (the C64's delay 255 - 12 x level).
'
' Liberties: the 40 x 23 field narrowed to 28 x 21 with the same plan
' (rooms five cells wide instead of eight, gaps on rows 5, 10 and 15, the
' pockets three cells long, 29 hearts in the same kinds of places); the
' butterflies' places staggered a row (rooms' left pair on rows 2 and 18,
' right pair on 3 and 17) so no more than four sprites share a line; smooth
' sprites gliding from cell to cell (the rules stay cell by cell, as the
' C64's), with an invisible mark tile under each butterfly in the name
' table (the C64 kept them on its screen too); the butterflies' step
' spread over four frames, four at a time, the last ones first (sixteen in
' one frame overran it); everything freezes while the raider bursts; a
' gate tile in the wall at the exit, opening when the last heart
' is taken; speeds for people: the butterflies step every 14 frames on
' level 1, a frame quicker every two levels down to 6 from level 17, the
' raider a cell in 8 frames on level 1, 6 frames quicker than them (5 at
' least), instead of the same pace
' (the C64 reached no delay at all by level 21); one player (the C64 took
' 1-9 players in turns; no keyboard here); the skill chosen with the
' stick on the title; the raider a fixed-colour figure (the C64's ball
' changed colour every move, too fast a flash here); the C64's random
' border colour each round left out; a short READY pause before each
' round; scores added as they happen (the C64 added them after each round,
' with the same totals); high score; GAME OVER returns to the title (no
' "ANOTHER GAME?").
' Standing still scores nothing. As on the C64, the heart in the start
' pocket keeps the butterflies out until it is taken (they never step onto
' hearts); after that the pocket is no refuge (they wander in and pounce),
' and stepping back and forth in one place is no safer.
'
' Controls: the joystick (either controller) moves the raider, diagonals
' too. On the title, left and right choose the skill, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.
' Nothing flashes faster than 3 Hz (the burst changes colour at 2.5 Hz).
'
' Workarounds for Amy bugs jake-return-before-for removed 2026-11-01.

